# What the knowledge graph should read, and what it should not.
#
# Measured before writing this: 2,643 KB of markdown, of which 74% is the
# playbook of a finished migration and 20% is tooling documentation. An
# extraction pass over the unscoped corpus spends ~94% of its budget on
# material that answers nothing about the harness as it exists today.
#
# The scoping mirrors the provenance tiers `tools/spec_code_bridge.py`
# already applies, so the two layers of the graph agree on what counts as an
# answer to "why does this exist":
#
#   openspec/specs/ + AGENTS.md + CLAUDE.md   -> contract   (kept)
#   openspec/changes/archive/                 -> rationale  (kept)
#   plans/, specs/                            -> superseded (excluded here)
#
# `openspec/changes/archive/` stays deliberately: those are the ADRs behind
# decisions still in force. `plans/` does not: `plans/roadmap.md` declares
# that migration finished, and two later SDD changes refactored past the code
# shape those slices describe. Keeping it made `plans/` outvote the standing
# contract 63:1 in the spec->code bridge before that tier existed.

